\v 1 In the year¦289351 \add before\add* the Assyrian King¦289358 Sargon¦289357 sent¦289355 his top army commander¦289353 to Ashdod¦289354, and he attacked and captured¦289362 it,
\v 2 Yahweh¦289367 told Yeshayah \add (Isaiah¦289369,\add* Amots' son), “Take off the sackcloth¦289376 and the sandals¦289379 \add ≈that you've been wearing\add*.” \add ≈So he started\add* walking¦289385 around naked¦289386\f + \fr 20:2 \ft This doesn't necessarily mean that he wore absolutely nothing.\f* and barefoot¦289387.
\v 3 \add ≈Later\add* Yahweh¦289391 said¦289390, “Just as my servant¦289394 Yeshayah¦289395 has been walking around naked¦289396 and barefoot¦289397 for three¦289398 years¦289399, it's a sign¦289400 and a \add ≈prophecy\add* about Egypt¦289404 \add (Heb. Mitsrayim)\add* and Ethiopia \add (Heb. Kush)\add*.
\v 4 It's a sign that the Assyrian king¦289411 will lead away captives¦289416 from Egypt and Ethiopia—young¦289422 men and old, barefoot¦289425 and naked¦289424 with their buttocks¦289427 exposed—to Egypt's¦289417 shame.
\v 5 They'll be discouraged¦289431 and ashamed¦289432 \add ≈because of\add* their \add unfounded\add* confidence¦289434 in Ethiopia and their boasting¦289438 about Egypt¦289437.
\v 6 Those who live \add ≈along those coastlines¦289442\add* will say, “Listen, we fled there for help¦289454 because that was our hope to \add ≈be saved\add* from the Assyrian king¦289457—how can we escape¦289460 \add now\add*?”
