\rem /s1 The Siege of Jerusalem; A Message about Jerusalem; The Fate of Jerusalem; Woe to David's City
\p
\v 1 \add ≈You won't end well\add* Ariel¦291811—the town¦291813 where¦291814 David¦291815 camped¦291814.
\q1 Year \add ≈follows\add* year—let the holidays come around¦291822,
\q1
\v 2 \add ≈but¦291824\add* I'll¦291824 bring¦291824 distress¦291824 to Ariel¦291825
\q1 and there'll be mourning¦291827 and wailing
\q1 and it'll become like an altar¦291831 hearth¦291831\f + \fr 29:2 \ft Here we use one of the possible translations of ‘ʼArīʼēl’ (rather than transliterating it as a name), but none of those three possible options seem to make a lot of sense.\f* to me.
\q1
\v 3 Then¦291833 I'll¦291833 \add get troops\add* \add ≈to camp all around you and besiege\add* you—
\q1 \add ≈building towers and ramps against your fortified¦291841 walls\add*.
\q1
\v 4 You'll be \add ≈brought down\add* and will speak¦291845 from the ground,
\q1 ≈and your \add ≈words will come\add* from the dust.
\q1 Your voice¦291852 will sound like a spirit speaking out of the ground,
\q1 and your speech will whisper¦291855 from the dust.
\p
\v 5 \add ≈But¦291857\add* the multitude of foreigners will be like fine dust,
\q1 ≈and that mass of ruthless¦291865 \add >people\add* will be like chaff¦291862 \add ≈that gets blown away\add*.
\q1 and it \add ≈will happen so suddenly¦291868\add*.
\q1
\v 6 Commander-in-chief Yahweh¦291871 \add ≈will send\add* thunder¦291874, an earthquake¦291875, and a loud noise,
\q1 a strong gale, a windstorm and a fire¦291881 \add ≈burning¦291881 things up\add*.
\q1
\v 7 It'll be like a dream¦291885—a vision¦291886 in the night¦291887.
